If an employee is selected at random from those who prefer vegetarian lunches, what is the probability that the employee works the afternoon shift?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$\\frac{29}{130}$"},{"key":"B","text":"$\\frac{29}{75}$"},{"key":"C","text":"$\\frac{29}{47}$"},{"key":"D","text":"$\\frac{47}{130}$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'This is a conditional probability question. Given that the selected employee prefers vegetarian lunches, the sample space is restricted to the 47 vegetarian employees. Of those 47, exactly 29 work the afternoon shift, so the conditional probability is $\\frac{29}{47}$. Using $\\frac{29}{130}$ incorrectly places 29 afternoon-vegetarian employees over the entire workforce instead of conditioning on vegetarian preference. Using $\\frac{29}{75}$ incorrectly uses the afternoon shift total as the denominator, confusing the conditioning event. The value $\\frac{47}{130}$ gives the overall probability of being vegetarian, which is not the conditional probability asked for.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);























Patterson soon recognized that his laboratory instruments were being contaminated by environmental lead at levels far exceeding natural background concentrations. Tracing this contamination to leaded gasoline and industrial emissions, he launched a decades-long campaign that ultimately led to the phaseout of leaded gasoline in the United States in 1986.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It describes how a methodological challenge encountered during Patterson's geological research redirected his career toward an environmental cause."},{"key":"B","text":"It argues that Patterson's age-of-Earth calculation was unreliable because his instruments were contaminated."},{"key":"C","text":"It provides additional technical details about the isotope measurement procedure described in the first paragraph."},{"key":"D","text":"It contrasts Patterson's geological contributions with those of other geochemists working on similar problems."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The first paragraph establishes Patterson''s geological achievement — dating the Earth. The second paragraph explains that contamination problems he encountered during that work led him to investigate environmental lead pollution, ultimately resulting in a policy campaign. The second paragraph thus shows how a challenge from his original research shifted his focus to environmental advocacy. The claim that it argues his calculation was unreliable overstates the implication: the first paragraph explicitly notes his figure remains accepted, and the contamination issue prompted a new direction rather than invalidating his result. The suggestion that it provides additional technical details is wrong because the second paragraph discusses contamination sources and a policy campaign, not measurement procedures. The idea that it contrasts Patterson with other geochemists is incorrect because no other scientists are mentioned.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






Historian Renata Sokolova has argued that the rapid adoption of mechanical clocks in fourteenth-century European monasteries was driven primarily by liturgical necessity. Monks needed to observe the canonical hours — the fixed daily cycle of prayer — with precision, and mechanical clocks provided a reliability that sundials and water clocks could not match during northern Europe''s long, overcast winters.

Text 2
Economic historian Jun Taniguchi contends that while monastic timekeeping created early demand for mechanical clocks, the technology''s proliferation beyond religious institutions was propelled by mercantile interests. Fourteenth-century textile guilds in Flanders and northern Italy adopted tower clocks to regulate workers'' hours, enforce production schedules, and coordinate market openings — functions that had no liturgical dimension. Taniguchi maintains that without this commercial appropriation, mechanical clocks would have remained a niche monastic tool rather than the organizing technology of urban life.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 extends the argument of Text 1 by attributing the broader social impact of mechanical clocks to economic forces that operated independently of the religious motivation Text 1 identifies."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 directly refutes Text 1 by providing evidence that monasteries did not actually adopt mechanical clocks during the fourteenth century."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 supports Text 1 by offering additional examples of monasteries that relied on mechanical clocks for liturgical purposes."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 undermines Text 1 by arguing that sundials and water clocks were in fact more reliable than mechanical clocks in overcast climates."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Taniguchi explicitly accepts Sokolova''s claim that monastic timekeeping created early demand for clocks, but he argues that the technology''s widespread adoption was driven by mercantile interests — textile guilds and market coordination — that had no liturgical dimension. Text 2 therefore extends rather than contradicts Text 1, identifying a separate economic mechanism responsible for the clocks'' broader societal role. The claim that Text 2 refutes Text 1 by denying monastic adoption is wrong because Taniguchi acknowledges monastic demand as the initial driver. The suggestion that Text 2 supports Text 1 with additional monastic examples is incorrect because Text 2 shifts focus to commercial uses outside monasteries. The idea that Text 2 undermines Text 1 by defending sundials and water clocks contradicts Taniguchi''s text, which does not discuss the reliability of earlier timekeeping devices.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Urban planner Elara Voss has proposed that cities should replace conventional streetlights with low-intensity amber lighting. She argues that amber wavelengths reduce disruption to the circadian rhythms of both humans and urban wildlife, making cities healthier environments for all inhabitants.

Text 2
A 2023 report by transportation safety researcher Tomasz Wojcik found that intersections illuminated with low-intensity amber lighting experienced a 23 percent increase in pedestrian-vehicle collisions compared to those with standard white LED lighting. Wojcik concluded that visibility is the primary safety variable in urban lighting design and should not be compromised for secondary benefits.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"With agreement, since both authors prioritize the health effects of lighting on urban wildlife."},{"key":"B","text":"With concern, since the data suggests that amber lighting may increase pedestrian safety risks."},{"key":"C","text":"With indifference, since Wojcik's research does not address circadian rhythm disruption."},{"key":"D","text":"With enthusiasm, since lower-intensity lighting would reduce municipal energy costs."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Wojcik''s research found that amber lighting correlated with a 23 percent increase in pedestrian-vehicle collisions and concluded that visibility should not be compromised for secondary benefits. He would therefore respond to Voss''s proposal with concern, as his data indicates her recommended lighting change poses safety risks. The suggestion that he would agree because both prioritize wildlife health is wrong because Wojcik''s focus is transportation safety, not wildlife. The claim that he would be indifferent because his research does not address circadian rhythms mischaracterizes his position: even though he does not study circadian effects, his finding directly challenges the viability of Voss''s proposal by identifying a safety cost, making indifference implausible. The idea that he would be enthusiastic about energy cost savings is unsupported because neither text discusses energy costs.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Marine ecologist Priya Chandrasekaran advocates for coral gardening — a technique in which fragments of resilient coral species are cultivated in underwater nurseries and then transplanted onto degraded reefs. She contends that gardening offers the most immediate and scalable path to rebuilding reef ecosystems, noting that transplanted corals can establish functional habitats within three to five years.

Text 2
Genomics researcher David Iwamura argues that coral gardening, while useful in the short term, does not address the fundamental vulnerability of reef ecosystems to rising ocean temperatures. He proposes that selective breeding programs targeting heat-tolerant genetic variants represent a more durable strategy, even though such programs require a decade or more to produce transplant-ready stock.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Chandrasekaran emphasizes the speed of habitat recovery, whereas Iwamura prioritizes long-term resilience to environmental change."},{"key":"B","text":"Chandrasekaran focuses on the economic costs of restoration, whereas Iwamura is concerned with the ecological diversity of reefs."},{"key":"C","text":"Chandrasekaran argues that coral species are not endangered, whereas Iwamura insists they face imminent extinction."},{"key":"D","text":"Chandrasekaran recommends abandoning degraded reefs, whereas Iwamura proposes protecting them from further damage."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Chandrasekaran advocates coral gardening because transplanted corals establish functional habitats within three to five years, emphasizing the speed of recovery. Iwamura acknowledges coral gardening''s short-term value but argues for selective breeding of heat-tolerant variants as a more durable strategy against rising temperatures, even though it takes longer. The key difference is that one prioritizes immediacy while the other prioritizes long-term thermal resilience. The claim about economic costs versus ecological diversity is wrong because neither author discusses costs or species diversity in these terms. The suggestion that Chandrasekaran denies corals are endangered while Iwamura asserts imminent extinction is unsupported by either text. The idea that Chandrasekaran recommends abandoning degraded reefs contradicts her proposal to transplant corals onto them.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Notes:
- The Salton Sea in southeastern California was accidentally created in 1905 when irrigation canals from the Colorado River breached and flooded the Salton Sink.
- By the 1950s, the Salton Sea had become a popular resort destination, attracting over 1.5 million visitors annually for boating, fishing, and swimming.
- Beginning in the 1980s, agricultural runoff increased the sea''s salinity and triggered algal blooms that caused massive fish die-offs.
- Today the receding shoreline exposes toxic dust composed of pesticide residue and heavy metals.
- Several restoration proposals have been submitted to the California state legislature since 2003.', E'While researching a topic for a local history journal, a student has compiled the following notes about the Salton Sea.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The Salton Sea, accidentally formed in 1905 when Colorado River irrigation canals breached, has been the subject of several restoration proposals submitted to California's legislature since 2003."},{"key":"B","text":"Once a thriving resort that drew over 1.5 million visitors a year for boating and swimming, the Salton Sea has deteriorated into an ecological crisis zone where rising salinity, fish die-offs, and toxic dust now define the landscape."},{"key":"C","text":"Agricultural runoff beginning in the 1980s increased the Salton Sea's salinity and triggered algal blooms, leading to massive fish die-offs along its shores."},{"key":"D","text":"Located in southeastern California, the Salton Sea was accidentally created in 1905 and by the 1950s had become a popular destination attracting over 1.5 million annual visitors."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to present a contrast between two phases of the Salton Sea''s history. The choice beginning with "Once a thriving resort" juxtaposes the prosperous 1950s resort era against the current ecological decline, directly fulfilling the contrastive goal. The option linking the sea''s accidental formation to restoration proposals spans the timeline but does not set up a contrast between two distinct phases. The option about agricultural runoff describes only the decline phase without contrasting it to the earlier prosperity. The option about the sea''s creation and 1950s popularity covers only the earlier, positive phase without addressing the subsequent deterioration.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







Notes:
- Glass frogs belong to the family Centrolenidae and are native to Central and South American cloud forests.
- Their dorsal (top) skin is typically lime green.
- Their ventral (underside) skin is translucent, making internal organs such as the heart, liver, and intestines visible.
- Over 150 species of glass frogs have been described by scientists.
- Glass frogs are primarily nocturnal and arboreal.', E'While researching a topic for a biology newsletter, a student has compiled the following notes about glass frogs.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Native to cloud forests in Central and South America, glass frogs belong to the family Centrolenidae, which contains over 150 described species."},{"key":"B","text":"Glass frogs are named for their remarkable translucent undersides, through which internal organs — including the heart, liver, and intestines — are plainly visible."},{"key":"C","text":"Glass frogs are primarily nocturnal and arboreal amphibians found throughout Central and South American cloud forests."},{"key":"D","text":"Scientists have described more than 150 species of glass frogs, all of which belong to the family Centrolenidae and inhabit cloud forests."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize what makes glass frogs visually distinctive. The choice mentioning their translucent undersides and visible internal organs directly highlights their unique visual feature, fulfilling the stated rhetorical goal. The option about taxonomy and species count focuses on classification rather than visual characteristics. The option about nocturnal and arboreal behavior describes habits, not visual traits. The option citing the number of described species and their habitat addresses biodiversity and geography, not visual distinctiveness.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jso