Notes:
- Mangrove forests are widely regarded as effective coastal buffers that reduce wave energy and protect inland areas from storm surges.
- A 2021 study by Phan et al. measured wave attenuation across 37 mangrove sites in Vietnam, the Philippines, and Bangladesh.
- The study found that mangrove belts narrower than 150 meters reduced wave height by only 11–19%, a margin the researchers deemed insufficient to prevent structural damage to buildings during severe typhoons.
- Wider mangrove belts (over 500 meters) reduced wave height by 50–70%.
- Coastal development has reduced average mangrove belt width in Southeast Asia from approximately 800 meters in 1990 to under 200 meters by 2020.', E'While preparing a research summary for an academic conference poster, a student has compiled the following notes about mangrove forests and coastal protection.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Mangrove forests are effective coastal buffers: a 2021 study by Phan et al. found that mangrove belts over 500 meters wide reduced wave height by 50–70% across 37 sites in Vietnam, the Philippines, and Bangladesh."},{"key":"B","text":"Although mangrove forests are widely considered reliable storm-surge barriers, a 2021 study by Phan et al. found that belts narrower than 150 meters — now typical in Southeast Asia, where average width has shrunk from roughly 800 meters to under 200 meters since 1990 — reduced wave height by only 11–19%, a margin the researchers considered insufficient to prevent structural damage during severe typhoons."},{"key":"C","text":"Coastal development in Southeast Asia has reduced average mangrove belt width from approximately 800 meters in 1990 to under 200 meters by 2020, raising concerns about the long-term viability of mangrove-based coastal protection strategies."},{"key":"D","text":"A 2021 study by Phan et al. examined wave attenuation across 37 mangrove sites in three countries and found that wider mangrove belts were substantially more effective than narrower ones at reducing wave height during storm events."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal requires qualifying a widely held assumption by presenting complicating evidence. The choice beginning with "Although mangrove forests are widely considered" explicitly names the common assumption (mangroves as reliable barriers), then introduces the Phan et al. finding that narrow belts — which are now the norm due to coastal development — provide inadequate protection. This structure directly qualifies the assumption with specific complicating evidence. The option presenting the 50–70% figure for wide belts reinforces rather than qualifies the assumption. The option about shrinking mangrove width addresses the development trend without connecting it to the protective-capacity data that complicates the assumption. The option summarizing the study''s width-effectiveness comparison presents a neutral finding without framing it as a qualification of any assumption.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);
